after years of insisting on the  importance of in-person learning we  classical education people are suddenly  forced into the world of online learning  and our job now is educating students  remotely and advising their parents  about how to do this work that we're  used to doing so first of all that's  very hard we miss our students and we're  used to being them being there with them  in person every day and it's hard to see  the success it's hard to see you know  how we can be productive in this new  environment we need to acknowledge first  that no matter what we do for the rest  of this semester it's not going to be a  substitute for what was going to happen  and I think that's okay to acknowledge  the loss acknowledge that it's a shame  but then move on to figure out what we  can do to make the most of this new  situation this is an opportunity for us  to remember that parents are the primary  educators of their children it makes  sense that when something terrible is  going on kids go home to their families  that's a sign of what we have always  known we love our students as teachers  and we take care of them and we love  watching them grow up but we are not  their parents their parents are their  parents and this challenging situation  is a reminder of that that means that as  teachers our work now all of a sudden is  to help the parents figure out how to do  the work that we were going to do in the  classrooms and that's a beautiful thing  it's a beautiful thing for parents to  become more involved in their children's  education than they ever have been let's  talk about ways to keep classical  education going even when your students  aren't physically with you in the  classroom part of the reason that in  classical education we think that  in-person learning is so important is  that there's kind of a community in the  classroom and there's a community in the  school and we're afraid that that  community is going to be lost there are  a few things that you can do to  encourage community so for example if  you have older students middle school  and high school students you can do  things like zoom meetings you can do  things like phone calls where your  you have a number of students on the  line maybe a small group is better than  a large group but something so that that  conversation that you're used to in the  classroom can still go on the other  thing that I think we should remember as  teachers is that this is a time to keep  it simple first of all acknowledge what  will be lost focus instead on what you  can do what opportunities you have with  these new challenging circumstances and  as you think about what plans you had  for the rest of the semester  focus on the things that are most  important don't think about quantity  think about quality think about what you  as a teacher need your students to come  away with at the end of the unit or the  end of the semester or the end of the  week and use the means that you have  before you whether it's sending home a  packet or emailing students or using  zoom or using phone calls use all of  those avenues that you have to be very  focused with the student and the parent  about what exactly the meat of the  lesson was what was the most important  thing that you wanted them to learn and  then finally remember that your job as a  teacher is not merely to deliver content  that's not what classical education is  about it's not about filling the minds  of these students with a whole bunch of  information it's about helping them  become a full human being now that means  different things for different people  but ultimately what it means is helping  our students learn to practice the  virtues helping them develop broad  understanding in the liberal arts and  sciences and then ultimately helping  them become thinking people who are able  to choose right and wrong I think if you  keep that final mission in your mind  during these challenging times it will  help you direct all of your lessons in  the right direction thank you and good  luck  you
 

